WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.467 --> 00:00:04.187
(spooky music)

00:00:04.187 --> 00:00:06.854
(birds crowing)

00:00:10.107 --> 00:00:10.940
- What?

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.130
Mike?

00:00:14.130 --> 00:00:16.083
- Yeah, hey, how are ya?

00:00:17.080 --> 00:00:18.760
This is fucking terrifying.

00:00:18.760 --> 00:00:19.593
- Are we...

00:00:20.480 --> 00:00:21.393
Are we gaming?

00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:23.090
- You're gaming.

00:00:23.090 --> 00:00:25.010
I'm just watching you through my fingers,

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.540
because that's kinda how I
handle this kind of stuff.

00:00:27.540 --> 00:00:29.650
And this is a scary fucking game.

00:00:29.650 --> 00:00:30.980
- This is everything I've ever dreamed of.

00:00:30.980 --> 00:00:34.370
I'm getting to play a video
game with Mike Flanagan.

00:00:34.370 --> 00:00:36.140
- Wow, that was the pinnacle, huh?

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:36.973
- This was it, yeah.

00:00:36.973 --> 00:00:37.980
- Sorry to hear that.

00:00:39.370 --> 00:00:42.010
- Oh, this is moody.

00:00:42.010 --> 00:00:43.310
- [Mike] Yeah.

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:45.610
- Do you still get scared?

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:46.980
- Oh yes, yes.

00:00:46.980 --> 00:00:49.950
And horror video games, in particular,

00:00:49.950 --> 00:00:51.799
is a big nope from me.

00:00:51.799 --> 00:00:52.632
- Oh.

00:00:52.632 --> 00:00:54.540
- Especially first-person stuff,

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.360
like, just, big nope.

00:00:56.360 --> 00:00:59.470
I used to watch my roommates play,

00:00:59.470 --> 00:01:02.180
I think it was Resident Evil
2, when I was in college,

00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:04.530
and we would watch it like
a movie, and we'd hide.

00:01:04.530 --> 00:01:05.964
But ever since I was a kid,

00:01:05.964 --> 00:01:08.550
I've always watched scary
stuff through my fingers.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:10.250
I get very uncomfortable with it.

00:01:10.250 --> 00:01:11.480
- So I was gonna ask you that.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.280
'Cause I remember, and I'm paraphrasing,

00:01:13.280 --> 00:01:15.090
but Tom Hardy said

00:01:15.090 --> 00:01:16.780
that the reason why he has an affinity

00:01:16.780 --> 00:01:18.020
for playing scary characters,

00:01:18.020 --> 00:01:20.570
like why he's good at it,
is because he's afraid.

00:01:20.570 --> 00:01:21.790
- Yeah.
- He's always afraid,

00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:23.080
so he's able to portray that.

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:23.930
Is that the case for you?

00:01:23.930 --> 00:01:26.950
Like, is it because you have fear

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.120
or you know what scares you

00:01:28.120 --> 00:01:30.540
that you're able to scare audiences?

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:31.700
Does that play into that?

00:01:31.700 --> 00:01:33.070
- I think maybe, yeah.

00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:34.890
I was a really--

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:35.800
Yup, there we go.

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.200
I was a really--

00:01:37.200 --> 00:01:38.650
That was just a fucking bird.

00:01:39.680 --> 00:01:42.270
I was a really scared kid.

00:01:42.270 --> 00:01:43.180
I was scared of everything.

00:01:43.180 --> 00:01:44.910
I was scared of my own shadow.

00:01:44.910 --> 00:01:45.743
- Oh wow.

00:01:45.743 --> 00:01:47.620
- I used to like hide behind the couch.

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.060
The first thing that gave me nightmares,

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:51.570
I remember it, was an
episode of "Fraggle Rock",

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.570
like scared the shit out of me.

00:01:53.570 --> 00:01:55.280
They had a thing called
the Terrible Tunnel

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:58.180
where all the ghosts of
Fraggles were trapped forever.

00:01:58.180 --> 00:01:59.550
- Yeah.
- It was really upsetting.

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:01.780
And so I got into horror,

00:02:01.780 --> 00:02:03.050
mostly books,

00:02:03.050 --> 00:02:04.030
Stephen King books,

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:05.350
because I was too scared to watch movies.

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:07.810
- Oh, so you just started with the novels.

00:02:07.810 --> 00:02:10.160
- I thought that would be less scary,

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:11.703
'cause it was all gonna be in my head.

00:02:11.703 --> 00:02:12.637
- Sure.
- I could control it.

00:02:12.637 --> 00:02:13.692
- That's a rookie--
- Opposite is true.

00:02:13.692 --> 00:02:16.510
- Yeah, that's a noob mistake right there.

00:02:16.510 --> 00:02:17.519
Oh.

00:02:17.519 --> 00:02:19.610
- But if I was like brave enough

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:21.270
to make it through a scary chapter

00:02:21.270 --> 00:02:22.690
or a scary movie

00:02:22.690 --> 00:02:24.523
or, like, a scary video game,

00:02:25.930 --> 00:02:29.810
then it was like a little
exercise in courage.

00:02:29.810 --> 00:02:30.790
- Sure.
- It was like going to the gym

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:33.800
and just doing a little
bit of exercise in bravery.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:34.633
- Yeah.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.475
- And so you kind of keep going--

00:02:37.475 --> 00:02:39.707
- [Video Game VO] Ethan...

00:02:39.707 --> 00:02:41.123
- [Video Game VO 2] What?

00:02:41.123 --> 00:02:42.830
- Don't like that.
- Yeah.

00:02:42.830 --> 00:02:44.130
You don't like horror at all, do you?

00:02:44.130 --> 00:02:44.963
- I don't.

00:02:44.963 --> 00:02:46.470
I was very angry with you.

00:02:46.470 --> 00:02:48.490
I don't know if you remember.

00:02:48.490 --> 00:02:50.550
After booking "Bly",

00:02:50.550 --> 00:02:53.423
and at my first meeting
with you in Vancouver,

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.640
literally within a minute,

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.960
I kind of had a go at you

00:02:57.960 --> 00:02:59.640
for the jump scare in "Hill House."

00:02:59.640 --> 00:03:00.790
- Yeah, in F8, yeah.

00:03:00.790 --> 00:03:01.750
- In the car.

00:03:01.750 --> 00:03:02.790
- Yeah, that's a good scare.

00:03:02.790 --> 00:03:05.060
- 'Cause I like doing research

00:03:05.060 --> 00:03:08.820
and having to rewatch your work

00:03:08.820 --> 00:03:09.993
was so scary.

00:03:09.993 --> 00:03:11.080
- [Video Game VO] There's
something I have to tell you.

00:03:11.080 --> 00:03:12.003
- [Video Game VO 2] Mia?

00:03:13.240 --> 00:03:14.120
- But it's funny though,

00:03:14.120 --> 00:03:15.200
'cause on the, like,

00:03:15.200 --> 00:03:16.240
when we were shooting "Bly",

00:03:16.240 --> 00:03:17.693
it never felt like we
were making a horror.

00:03:17.693 --> 00:03:19.900
- No, and that's the
thing about making horror

00:03:19.900 --> 00:03:21.480
is it never feels like horror.
- No.

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:23.740
- No, it feels like comedy,

00:03:23.740 --> 00:03:24.810
and it's all the same rules.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:25.940
It's rhythm

00:03:25.940 --> 00:03:27.100
and timing

00:03:27.100 --> 00:03:28.560
and everyone's having a great time,

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:29.740
'cause you break out the blood

00:03:29.740 --> 00:03:31.880
and the makeup and everything.

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:32.890
It's really fun.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:33.723
- Yeah.

00:03:33.723 --> 00:03:34.681
- But I think that's--

00:03:34.681 --> 00:03:35.514
(video game whispers)

00:03:35.514 --> 00:03:36.890
I'm sorry for you

00:03:36.890 --> 00:03:41.310
that you were uncomfortable
starting "Bly", but--

00:03:41.310 --> 00:03:42.143
- Even the scripts.

00:03:42.143 --> 00:03:44.170
I remember sitting there
reading the scripts,

00:03:45.480 --> 00:03:46.800
and I put the lights on.

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:49.210
I remember having to put the
lights on in the apartment.

00:03:49.210 --> 00:03:51.450
I'm just, it's an overactive imagination,

00:03:51.450 --> 00:03:52.283
I guess.
- Yeah.

00:03:52.283 --> 00:03:54.170
- [Video Game Woman] Everyone leaves me.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:55.370
- You know what else it is?

00:03:55.370 --> 00:03:58.709
It's an expression of your empathy.

00:03:58.709 --> 00:03:59.542
- Huh.

00:03:59.542 --> 00:04:00.940
- Because when you get
scared watching someone else

00:04:00.940 --> 00:04:01.950
in a scary situation,

00:04:01.950 --> 00:04:05.163
it's because you've put
yourself into their shoes.

00:04:06.150 --> 00:04:06.983
- Which is handy

00:04:06.983 --> 00:04:08.730
if you're an actor.

00:04:08.730 --> 00:04:10.250
- Very handy if you're an actor,

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:13.440
and I think handy just to be a human.

00:04:13.440 --> 00:04:15.770
But yeah, that's the
beautiful thing about horror.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:18.900
It lets us be brave

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.570
in a very controlled way.

00:04:20.570 --> 00:04:22.228
And the more we expose ourselves to it,

00:04:22.228 --> 00:04:24.830
the more courage we kind of grow.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:25.663
- Yeah.

00:04:25.663 --> 00:04:28.030
- And the more we get used to

00:04:28.030 --> 00:04:29.790
worrying for other people

00:04:29.790 --> 00:04:31.030
and their safety

00:04:31.030 --> 00:04:32.350
and being able to see through their eyes.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:35.973
So I think horror has a
really beautiful benefit

00:04:35.973 --> 00:04:36.910
to the world.

00:04:36.910 --> 00:04:38.580
It's also just really fucking fun.

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:39.413
- Sure.

00:04:39.413 --> 00:04:40.630
- Like it's fun to be scared.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:41.910
- Do you--

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:42.785
- Jesus.

00:04:42.785 --> 00:04:43.618
(Rahul laughs)

00:04:43.618 --> 00:04:44.740
- Yeah, this is intense.
- Yeah.

00:04:44.740 --> 00:04:46.590
- I feel like I'm already a bit lost.

00:04:48.473 --> 00:04:49.900
But obviously you've done...

00:04:51.140 --> 00:04:53.720
with regards to horror, like TV and film.

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:55.110
- Yeah.

00:04:55.110 --> 00:04:56.620
- When it comes to building tension,

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:57.580
jump scares, things like that,

00:04:57.580 --> 00:04:59.590
like how do you manage that

00:04:59.590 --> 00:05:02.040
across a season versus a two-hour movie?

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:06.590
Like how do you build tension
across a 10-episode arc?

00:05:06.590 --> 00:05:07.770
- Much harder in television,

00:05:07.770 --> 00:05:09.300
but much more fun.

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:11.180
I prefer television that way,

00:05:11.180 --> 00:05:14.135
'cause a movie, you're
just kind of getting going

00:05:14.135 --> 00:05:15.190
with that tension,

00:05:15.190 --> 00:05:16.830
and then it dissipates,

00:05:16.830 --> 00:05:18.380
and the jump scares, I hate jump scares.

00:05:18.380 --> 00:05:19.213
I hate 'em.

00:05:19.213 --> 00:05:20.046
- Oh really?

00:05:20.046 --> 00:05:20.879
- To this day.

00:05:22.290 --> 00:05:24.300
They're cheap shots, you know?
- Yeah.

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:26.615
- And they evaporate tension.

00:05:26.615 --> 00:05:29.916
More often than anything, people laugh--

00:05:29.916 --> 00:05:30.818
- [Video Game Man] What's going on?

00:05:30.818 --> 00:05:31.651
- When they start.

00:05:31.651 --> 00:05:32.572
- 'Cause it just breaks,
'cause they're pent up.

00:05:32.572 --> 00:05:33.687
- Yeah, it just breaks that whole thing.

00:05:33.687 --> 00:05:35.450
And that takes all that
beautiful atmosphere

00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:36.550
and tension you've built,

00:05:36.550 --> 00:05:38.900
and it completely lets the
air out of the balloon,

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:43.300
and everybody's too busy
laughing to be scared.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:45.560
See, this would wreck me.

00:05:45.560 --> 00:05:46.950
- Yeah, I'm like...

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.663
Video games for me, like,

00:05:50.750 --> 00:05:53.040
there's a passive experience,

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:55.900
which is when you're
watching a TV show or a film.

00:05:55.900 --> 00:05:56.733
- Yeah.

00:05:56.733 --> 00:05:58.100
- It's happening whether
you like it or not.

00:05:58.100 --> 00:05:59.240
- Yeah, you're on a ride.

00:05:59.240 --> 00:06:00.073
- You're on a ride.

00:06:00.073 --> 00:06:02.220
This is forcing you to turn the corner

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:03.340
you don't wanna turn.

00:06:03.340 --> 00:06:04.173
- Yup.

00:06:04.173 --> 00:06:05.267
And you know,

00:06:05.267 --> 00:06:08.060
and that's the anticipation
of it is always worse.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:09.830
- Oh God.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:10.710
This is creepy.

00:06:10.710 --> 00:06:11.810
- Yeah, this is great.

00:06:13.320 --> 00:06:14.220
But yeah.

00:06:14.220 --> 00:06:16.870
I think for long form,

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:19.610
it's really tough to
build enough scares in,

00:06:19.610 --> 00:06:21.963
because you are deflating
tension as you go.

00:06:21.963 --> 00:06:23.571
(creepy music)

00:06:23.571 --> 00:06:24.687
That's unfortunate.

00:06:24.687 --> 00:06:27.256
(Rahul laughs)

00:06:27.256 --> 00:06:30.570
And with a movie, so often
it's just about a scare

00:06:30.570 --> 00:06:32.020
every five minutes or ten minutes.

00:06:32.020 --> 00:06:33.890
It becomes really kind of metered out.

00:06:33.890 --> 00:06:35.840
But I think for me, what I love about it

00:06:35.840 --> 00:06:40.464
is having time to spend with
characters and atmosphere.

00:06:40.464 --> 00:06:41.297
- You care about them, right?

00:06:41.297 --> 00:06:42.250
- [Mike] I want that
over scares every time.

00:06:42.250 --> 00:06:43.940
Yeah.
- And you're more invested.

00:06:43.940 --> 00:06:46.580
- Yeah, if you care about
people, the scares land.

00:06:46.580 --> 00:06:49.650
Otherwise it's just, you're
walking up behind people

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.030
and banging things behind their head.

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.262
- Sure.
- And startling them,

00:06:53.262 --> 00:06:54.400
and that's fun, but.

00:06:54.400 --> 00:06:55.233
- Am I right?

00:06:55.233 --> 00:06:56.783
Did I imagine this?

00:06:56.783 --> 00:06:58.650
Is your favorite movie "Jaws"?

00:06:58.650 --> 00:07:00.020
Was that your favorite horror movie?

00:07:00.020 --> 00:07:00.853
Did I get that right?

00:07:00.853 --> 00:07:01.686
- Yeah, that's right.

00:07:01.686 --> 00:07:02.519
- Nailed it.

00:07:02.519 --> 00:07:03.352
- Yep, mmhmm.

00:07:03.352 --> 00:07:04.920
- How old were you when
you first saw "Jaws"?

00:07:04.920 --> 00:07:05.753
- I was a kid,

00:07:05.753 --> 00:07:08.310
and I've probably seen that
movie 200 times in my life.

00:07:08.310 --> 00:07:12.560
Like I learned to appreciate filmmaking

00:07:12.560 --> 00:07:13.610
just watching that movie

00:07:13.610 --> 00:07:14.712
over and over again as a kid.

00:07:14.712 --> 00:07:15.545
- Yep.

00:07:15.545 --> 00:07:16.930
- And realizing like, oh my God,

00:07:16.930 --> 00:07:18.560
these shots are really well-composed,

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.020
and they cut together in
a really specific way.

00:07:21.020 --> 00:07:21.988
- That was your first awareness

00:07:21.988 --> 00:07:24.727
of the filmmaking process?
- Yeah.

00:07:24.727 --> 00:07:26.300
And then I thought I knew that movie

00:07:26.300 --> 00:07:27.630
like the back of my hand,

00:07:27.630 --> 00:07:30.239
but I'd only ever seen it on VHS.

00:07:30.239 --> 00:07:31.919
- Oh.
- Four by three.

00:07:31.919 --> 00:07:33.220
- Yeah, yeah, yeah, yes.

00:07:33.220 --> 00:07:34.990
So you hadn't seen it framed.
- I finally saw it letterboxed

00:07:34.990 --> 00:07:36.867
to where they had restored

00:07:36.867 --> 00:07:38.420
the whole picture.

00:07:38.420 --> 00:07:40.830
And I felt like I was seeing
the movie for the first time.

00:07:40.830 --> 00:07:42.830
But, yeah, I learned a lot
about movies from that.

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:45.080
- Why is the biggest
scare in that movie --

00:07:45.080 --> 00:07:46.560
And it's one of those.

00:07:46.560 --> 00:07:48.860
That jump scare where they're under water

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:50.460
and the body--
- Yeah.

00:07:50.460 --> 00:07:52.710
- Comes up, the whole--
- Ben Gardner.

00:07:52.710 --> 00:07:54.160
- Yeah.
- His head pops through the--

00:07:54.160 --> 00:07:55.250
- No matter how many times I've seen it,

00:07:55.250 --> 00:07:56.670
I can't time...

00:07:57.610 --> 00:07:58.443
the jump scare.

00:07:58.443 --> 00:08:00.223
I cannot prepare mentally for it.

00:08:01.160 --> 00:08:02.160
- It's pretty great.

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:04.360
Neat trick with that.

00:08:04.360 --> 00:08:07.640
He brings all the other
sounds down to almost nothing

00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:08.580
right before that happens.

00:08:08.580 --> 00:08:11.430
And they had played the shark theme,

00:08:11.430 --> 00:08:13.190
and Dreyfuss is looking around,

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:14.960
and it's like you're expecting
the shark from behind him.

00:08:14.960 --> 00:08:16.920
And then all the music drops away,

00:08:16.920 --> 00:08:20.166
so he makes a bed of
silence right before it.

00:08:20.166 --> 00:08:23.680
And you see the head
start to rock into frame

00:08:25.190 --> 00:08:28.590
about a half second before you
hear the big musical sting,

00:08:28.590 --> 00:08:31.760
which is a technique that
that movie does beautifully.

00:08:31.760 --> 00:08:32.740
A lot of movies forget it.

00:08:32.740 --> 00:08:34.140
They put the big, loud noise

00:08:34.140 --> 00:08:36.870
right on the first frame of the thing,

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:39.110
but if you let the visual hit your brain--

00:08:39.110 --> 00:08:39.970
- And then there's that delay.

00:08:39.970 --> 00:08:41.000
- And as you react,

00:08:41.000 --> 00:08:43.450
you get hit with the audio
a split second later,

00:08:43.450 --> 00:08:45.830
it's incredibly effective.

00:08:45.830 --> 00:08:47.340
- Huh.
- But yeah.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:49.120
- Would you ever...

00:08:49.120 --> 00:08:51.260
Would you ever take your
talents to the video game world?

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:52.375
Would you?
- I would love to.

00:08:52.375 --> 00:08:53.859
I would absolutely love.

00:08:53.859 --> 00:08:54.692
(Rahul groans)

00:08:54.692 --> 00:08:55.910
- See that's what I don't like.

00:08:55.910 --> 00:08:56.743
I have to--

00:08:56.743 --> 00:08:57.616
- Having to go around the corner.

00:08:57.616 --> 00:08:58.449
- I have to pan this camera.

00:08:58.449 --> 00:08:59.282
- Yeah.

00:08:59.282 --> 00:09:00.247
- Myself.
- Yep.

00:09:00.247 --> 00:09:01.080
- And...

00:09:02.210 --> 00:09:03.513
For God's sake.

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:07.210
It's so unsettling.

00:09:07.210 --> 00:09:08.052
- It is.

00:09:08.052 --> 00:09:09.357
(Rahul groans)

00:09:09.357 --> 00:09:10.190
(door shuts)

00:09:10.190 --> 00:09:13.130
- Forcing you to participate
in it is amazing.

00:09:13.130 --> 00:09:14.680
And it's fun to watch for me.

00:09:14.680 --> 00:09:18.200
Like I'm enjoying watching you

00:09:18.200 --> 00:09:20.650
as this terrified--
- This is how I watched--

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:21.570
- Man-child trying to play this game.

00:09:21.570 --> 00:09:23.533
- Well, this is how I
watched all your work.

00:09:23.533 --> 00:09:24.910
Like this is how I do research.

00:09:24.910 --> 00:09:25.743
It's that.

00:09:25.743 --> 00:09:27.680
I pick a frame of the TV,

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:29.700
so whenever you've got
stuff going on there,

00:09:29.700 --> 00:09:31.220
so it looks like I'm in that direction.

00:09:31.220 --> 00:09:32.490
- But you can look off there.
- I'm cheating.

00:09:32.490 --> 00:09:34.067
- Yeah, very smart.

00:09:34.067 --> 00:09:34.900
- Oh.

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:38.587
We had a this-and-that

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:41.150
that was provided.

00:09:41.150 --> 00:09:42.350
Okay, are you ready?

00:09:42.350 --> 00:09:43.190
- [Mike] Yeah, I'm ready.

00:09:43.190 --> 00:09:44.361
- Freddy or Jason, who's scarier?

00:09:44.361 --> 00:09:45.194
- Freddy.

00:09:45.194 --> 00:09:47.260
- Ooh, "Alien" or "Predator"?

00:09:47.260 --> 00:09:48.700
- Alien.

00:09:48.700 --> 00:09:50.070
- Werewolves or vampires?

00:09:50.070 --> 00:09:51.720
- Vampires.

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:53.680
- Frankenstein or Dracula?

00:09:53.680 --> 00:09:54.513
- Dracula.

00:09:54.513 --> 00:09:56.715
But do you mean the monster or Dracula?

00:09:56.715 --> 00:09:57.961
- It would be Frankenstein's--

00:09:57.961 --> 00:09:58.794
- Or Dr. Frankenstein?

00:09:58.794 --> 00:09:59.987
- It could be Frankenstein's
monster, right?

00:09:59.987 --> 00:10:00.820
- Frankenstein's monster or Dracula.

00:10:00.820 --> 00:10:01.840
I'm gonna say Dracula.

00:10:02.998 --> 00:10:04.480
(Rahul laughs)

00:10:04.480 --> 00:10:07.461
- I know that, "Hill
House" or "Bly Manor"?

00:10:07.461 --> 00:10:08.770
(Mike laughs)

00:10:08.770 --> 00:10:09.780
- Oh, man.

00:10:09.780 --> 00:10:11.062
That's unfair.
- That is unfair.

00:10:11.062 --> 00:10:11.895
- That's really unfair.

00:10:11.895 --> 00:10:13.010
- That's like me asking you
to pick a favorite child.

00:10:13.010 --> 00:10:14.990
- You just Sophie's Choiced me.

00:10:14.990 --> 00:10:16.050
Um...

00:10:18.090 --> 00:10:20.270
I'm gonna say, for me, "Hill House".

00:10:20.270 --> 00:10:21.470
- Mm.
- Yeah.

00:10:21.470 --> 00:10:22.651
- Same.
- Yeah?

00:10:22.651 --> 00:10:23.610
- Yeah.

00:10:23.610 --> 00:10:26.600
Well, obviously I have affection for both.

00:10:26.600 --> 00:10:28.230
- Yeah.
- But "Hill House",

00:10:28.230 --> 00:10:30.360
I think 'cause I was a
bit more disconnected,

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.440
because I wasn't part of that one.

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:33.690
- [Mike] Yeah.

00:10:33.690 --> 00:10:35.190
- It broke me,

00:10:35.190 --> 00:10:38.160
and it was, yeah, it was phenomenal.

00:10:38.160 --> 00:10:39.890
But I also have so much
affection for "Bly",

00:10:39.890 --> 00:10:41.840
because of the Miles and Flora
are like family, and then.

00:10:41.840 --> 00:10:43.140
- Oh they're wonderful.

00:10:43.140 --> 00:10:44.250
Yeah.

00:10:44.250 --> 00:10:47.907
No, I love both of the
shows for different reasons.

00:10:47.907 --> 00:10:50.800
"Hill House" is always gonna
be really special to me.

00:10:50.800 --> 00:10:52.050
- [Rahul] Yeah.

00:10:52.940 --> 00:10:55.367
- But "Bly", you know,

00:10:55.367 --> 00:10:57.290
"Bly" is where I met you.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.460
And now we're just working
together all the time.

00:10:59.460 --> 00:11:00.293
- We are.

00:11:00.293 --> 00:11:02.560
- Which is such a delightful thing.

00:11:02.560 --> 00:11:05.490
- And we have something very,

00:11:05.490 --> 00:11:10.090
very, very special coming at some point,

00:11:10.090 --> 00:11:11.350
which we can't talk about right now,

00:11:11.350 --> 00:11:13.160
but "Midnight Mass".

00:11:13.160 --> 00:11:14.170
- Yes.

00:11:14.170 --> 00:11:16.013
Yeah, we don't wanna spoil that,

00:11:17.254 --> 00:11:19.500
but that was an amazing, amazing thing.

00:11:19.500 --> 00:11:21.250
It's my favorite.

00:11:21.250 --> 00:11:23.760
If you were to ask me these
questions a year from now,

00:11:23.760 --> 00:11:25.870
and it's like, that's my favorite

00:11:25.870 --> 00:11:27.120
I think I've gotten to work on.

00:11:27.120 --> 00:11:28.110
- Yeah.

00:11:28.110 --> 00:11:31.108
- [Mike] And you're fucking
great on that show, so...

00:11:31.108 --> 00:11:33.340
- Aww, Mike.
- It's gonna be really cool.

00:11:33.340 --> 00:11:34.180
- Um...

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:35.953
Yeah.

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:39.510
That show as well was like...

00:11:40.470 --> 00:11:43.750
We, obviously, we filmed
during the pandemic.

00:11:43.750 --> 00:11:44.619
- Yeah.

00:11:44.619 --> 00:11:45.452
- Like we were one of the first.

00:11:45.452 --> 00:11:47.310
- We were among the very
first shows in the world

00:11:47.310 --> 00:11:49.330
to go up during the pandemic, yeah.

00:11:49.330 --> 00:11:50.163
- Yeah.

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:53.760
- And that was terrifying.

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:55.180
That was--

00:11:55.180 --> 00:11:56.988
- It was uncharted territory.
- Yeah.

00:11:56.988 --> 00:11:58.500
The protocols were brand new.

00:11:58.500 --> 00:12:01.170
We were kind of the test case,

00:12:01.170 --> 00:12:04.650
and we made it through
the whole experience.

00:12:04.650 --> 00:12:05.960
We never shut down one day.

00:12:05.960 --> 00:12:07.240
- Nope.
- Nope.

00:12:07.240 --> 00:12:09.000
- It became--
- It was 83 days of shooting.

00:12:09.000 --> 00:12:10.590
- We were the guinea pigs.

00:12:10.590 --> 00:12:11.423
- [Mike] Yeah.

00:12:13.244 --> 00:12:14.077
- Here we go.

00:12:15.670 --> 00:12:18.433
All right, I'm gonna do this.

00:12:19.430 --> 00:12:21.360
If you...

00:12:21.360 --> 00:12:24.263
Like, obviously you're known for horror.

00:12:25.620 --> 00:12:28.800
Is there a genre that you would love to.

00:12:28.800 --> 00:12:32.152
like if you were given any,
like, complete free range,

00:12:32.152 --> 00:12:34.820
is there a genre that you'd
love to tackle that you haven't?

00:12:34.820 --> 00:12:35.960
- Oh, yeah.

00:12:35.960 --> 00:12:40.630
No, look, I'd love to make
a huge sci-fi project.

00:12:40.630 --> 00:12:41.503
I'd love to.

00:12:42.490 --> 00:12:44.294
- Without horror elements?
- Without horror elements.

00:12:44.294 --> 00:12:45.127
- You wouldn't go "Alien" with it?

00:12:45.127 --> 00:12:46.033
- Proper sci-fi.
- Oh.

00:12:47.150 --> 00:12:50.280
- I've always wanted to do a family film.

00:12:50.280 --> 00:12:53.946
My kids can't see any
single thing I've ever done.

00:12:53.946 --> 00:12:55.030
- [Rahul] (laughing) Yeah.

00:12:55.030 --> 00:12:57.580
- I would love to do
something my kids could watch.

00:12:58.730 --> 00:13:01.623
And I've always really
wanted to do a Western.

00:13:03.670 --> 00:13:05.244
- Mike, that's my favorite.

00:13:05.244 --> 00:13:06.860
- [Mike] Yeah, I think
that would be a blast.

00:13:06.860 --> 00:13:07.693
- [Rahul] Yeah.

00:13:09.230 --> 00:13:10.910
I feel like this is getting...

00:13:12.736 --> 00:13:13.569
- You're doomed.

00:13:13.569 --> 00:13:14.920
I mean something.

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:15.920
- I don't like this.

00:13:19.550 --> 00:13:20.470
Ah!

00:13:20.470 --> 00:13:21.303
Okay.

00:13:23.240 --> 00:13:24.430
We have a doll.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:25.263
- Yeah.

00:13:26.476 --> 00:13:28.385
(video game screams)

00:13:28.385 --> 00:13:31.410
- [Video Game Doll] I've
been waiting for so long.

00:13:31.410 --> 00:13:34.450
I'd make a much better daughter than Rose.

00:13:34.450 --> 00:13:37.804
Please, won't you stay with me...

00:13:37.804 --> 00:13:38.637
Forever?

00:13:39.586 --> 00:13:41.842
(video game doll cackles)

00:13:41.842 --> 00:13:42.950
- What?

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:44.498
Are we?

00:13:44.498 --> 00:13:45.746
Oh, the doll's gone.
- Looks like the doll--

00:13:45.746 --> 00:13:47.094
- Oh, there's a bigger one.

00:13:47.094 --> 00:13:49.170
- Yeah, human-sized doll.

00:13:49.170 --> 00:13:50.003
- Oh.

00:13:51.911 --> 00:13:52.780
There we go.

00:13:52.780 --> 00:13:53.990
Do I need to...

00:13:56.670 --> 00:13:57.820
Guess I'm examining it.

00:13:59.860 --> 00:14:01.960
- Now I'm just getting
sucked into the game.

00:14:01.960 --> 00:14:02.859
- That's the problem with game plays.

00:14:02.859 --> 00:14:04.520
- Yeah.

00:14:04.520 --> 00:14:06.040
- It just becomes about that.

00:14:06.040 --> 00:14:07.570
I don't, am I.

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:10.153
Why do I feel like this is
gonna spring up in a second?

00:14:12.300 --> 00:14:13.723
I don't know what I'm doing here.

00:14:13.723 --> 00:14:16.223
(clock ticks)

00:14:26.510 --> 00:14:28.700
- One of the things I find so
scary about games like this

00:14:28.700 --> 00:14:30.540
is that you get completely

00:14:30.540 --> 00:14:32.550
just stuck in the world

00:14:32.550 --> 00:14:34.350
and unless you advance yourself forward,

00:14:34.350 --> 00:14:35.580
you're never getting out, right?

00:14:35.580 --> 00:14:36.413
- Right.

00:14:36.413 --> 00:14:38.030
- Like you're stuck here.

00:14:38.030 --> 00:14:39.900
And I think I'm wondering

00:14:39.900 --> 00:14:42.770
what it is about your life right now

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:45.630
that's made you dream this,

00:14:45.630 --> 00:14:49.890
and I think it's because
you're fully trapped now

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:51.650
in my world,

00:14:51.650 --> 00:14:55.000
like you are stuck in
the Intrepid universe,

00:14:55.000 --> 00:14:57.710
because for someone
who hates horror shows,

00:14:57.710 --> 00:15:02.710
you're just gonna be cast in
them again and again and again.

00:15:02.800 --> 00:15:05.733
And there's really no escape
for you at this point.

00:15:07.860 --> 00:15:10.340
- That's one way to look at it, yeah.

00:15:10.340 --> 00:15:13.080
- Yeah, I think it's probably time now

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:14.690
for you to wake up.

00:15:14.690 --> 00:15:15.810
Wake up.

00:15:15.810 --> 00:15:17.210
Rahul, wake up.

00:15:17.210 --> 00:15:18.528
Wake up.

00:15:18.528 --> 00:15:21.327
(dream shimmers)

